WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.240
The first film followed blue DNA into green messenger RNA and a red amino acid

2
00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:11.260
chain. If a DNA letter changes, how much of that chain changes?

3
00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:14.860
Keep watching the same blocks and reading window.

4
00:00:18.383 --> 00:00:21.463
A mutation is a change in DNA base order.

5
00:00:22.763 --> 00:00:24.483
A substitution swaps a base.

6
00:00:25.703 --> 00:00:30.463
Because several codons can mean the same amino acid, a swap can leave the chain

7
00:00:30.463 --> 00:00:36.343
unchanged. Another swap can change an amino acid or create an early stop.

8
00:00:37.383 --> 00:00:41.143
An insertion adds bases, and a deletion removes them.

9
00:00:42.323 --> 00:00:45.083
The ribosome does not see spaces between codons.

10
00:00:46.363 --> 00:00:49.643
It starts at its reading point and groups the message from there.

11
00:00:50.903 --> 00:00:54.903
Adding or removing one base inside the coding part shifts that grouping.

12
00:00:56.183 --> 00:00:57.483
This is called a frameshift.

13
00:00:58.567 --> 00:01:02.347
Predict what happens when the waiting block enters near the start.

14
00:01:03.687 --> 00:01:07.347
Will only its codon change, or will later codons change too?

15
00:01:08.607 --> 00:01:10.367
Follow the dividers, not the colours.

16
00:01:16.417 --> 00:01:19.717
The inserted block shifts every divider after it.

17
00:01:20.837 --> 00:01:24.277
The gold ribosome now reads new codons from that point.

18
00:01:25.617 --> 00:01:29.697
Many later amino acids can change, and an early stop may appear.

19
00:01:31.037 --> 00:01:33.577
Removing a base can shift the frame in the same way.

20
00:01:34.567 --> 00:01:36.627
Now compare a quieter substitution.

21
00:01:38.547 --> 00:01:41.187
Messenger codon GAG means glutamic acid.

22
00:01:42.567 --> 00:01:46.927
Change its last letter, making GAA, and it still means glutamic acid.

23
00:01:48.127 --> 00:01:51.687
The DNA changed, but this amino acid chain did not.

24
00:01:52.967 --> 00:01:55.187
This outcome is called a silent mutation.

25
00:01:56.267 --> 00:01:58.327
Now follow the sickle substitution.

26
00:01:59.827 --> 00:02:02.087
The usual coding DNA reads GAG.

27
00:02:03.227 --> 00:02:05.407
The sickle allele reads GTG.

28
00:02:06.767 --> 00:02:10.527
Messenger RNA therefore reads GUG instead of GAG.

29
00:02:10.527 --> 00:02:15.127
The ribosome places valine instead of glutamic acid.

30
00:02:16.167 --> 00:02:19.467
That changed spot can alter hemoglobin's behaviour.

31
00:02:20.887 --> 00:02:24.787
When oxygen is low, sickle hemoglobin can join into stiff rods.

32
00:02:26.127 --> 00:02:28.287
The rods can bend a soft red blood cell.

33
00:02:29.547 --> 00:02:31.547
A letter change has reached a whole cell.

34
00:02:32.567 --> 00:02:35.667
A changed letter does not guarantee a changed trait.

35
00:02:36.927 --> 00:02:39.827
Some substitutions keep the same amino acid.

36
00:02:41.147 --> 00:02:45.607
Protein effects also depend on which copies a person has and the environment.

37
00:02:47.007 --> 00:02:48.687
Here is the reading rule to keep.

38
00:02:49.907 --> 00:02:54.927
In coding DNA, swaps change one codon; frameshifts change later codons.
